  "query_preview": "..\nVisible satellite and radar imagery shows the center of the MCV is\npassing west of Flagstaff, near Williams, and moving in a north-\nnorthwest direction, producing a quick burst of showers and\nthunderstorms, and then leaving behind a layer of mid- to upper-\nlevel cloud cover. This cloud layer is helping to suppress new \nconvection across Prescott, Sedona, Flagstaff, and surrounding \nareas this afternoon. However, areas that have remain mostly clear\nthis morning, especially areas north to northwest of Flagstaff, \nnear the Grand Canyon, and eastern portions of the CWA, including \nmost of Navajo and Apache counties, are starting to see new \nshowers and thunderstorms develop near higher terrain and where \ndaytime heating was the greatest today. This activity is expected \nto continue through the rest of the afternoon and early evening \nhours before settling down tonight.\n\nFor Wednesday onward, the high pressure that was over New Mexico\nwill strengthen and shift westward and over Arizona, leading to \nan overall decrease in showers and thunderstorms as subsidence \nincreases and drier, mid-level air moves into the region. \nHowever, this will not completely stop the low-level monsoonal \nmoisture, so isolated to low-end scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will still be possible over the higher terrain \nduring the late morning and afternoon hours. The decrease in \nMonsoon, along with the strengthening and relocation of the area \nof high pressure, will cause temperatures to rise above normal by \nThursday, with a more significant rise for Friday through Sunday. \nAn Extreme Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon (below 4000 ft) will \nstart at 10 MST Thursday, with additional warnings starting 10 \nMST/ 11 AM MDT Friday for northern Gila County (below 4000 ft), \nMarble and Glen Canyon, including the city of Page, and Oak Creek \nand Sycamore Canyons, including the city of Sedona.",
